A||N||1||fogglethorpe||In defense of missile defense...||||4||
Z||000000||fogglethorpe||12-10-2001||09:59 PM||||9/11/01 is more than a wakeup call for our nation. It once would have been preposterous to suppose that carnage of that magnitude could occur here, so quickly, and by such bizarre means. Even now, as we nurse our wounds, and seek vengeance and victory, we must come to grips with our utter vulnerability.<BR>   Had our enemy possessed the capability on that day to launch nuclear strikes against a city like New York, we would now see all of Manhattan in rubble, and death tolls in the hundreds of thousands. It is only a matter of time before some mad, third-world thug  with a bone to pick against the west will possess such capabilities, and use them liberally.<BR>   China, a much more formidable enemy, currently has at least 20 ICBMs aimed at our mainland.<BR>   Why, then, is our government unwilling to fulfill its primary Constitutional duty to "provide for the common defense" by dragging its feet on missile defense? The answer is simple: The Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty of 1972, signed by the United States and the now defunct Soviet Union. This treaty, very basically, prohibits national missile defense for both countries.<BR>   The Soviets violated this treaty before the ink was dry, and today possess the world's only operable missile defense system. A treaty violated is a treaty null and void, and our adherence to this one could be to our peril.<BR>   Would our implementation of a missile defense ignite another arms race?<BR>Maybe, but the Soviet Union learned a hard lesson in the 1980s when their<BR>economy collapsed and their country broke up like a dropped vase. Neither <BR>Russia nor China would be able to compete with us today, much less their<BR>proxies.<BR>   Will our leaders do the right thing before the window of opportunity closes, and we are geopolitically blackmailed by some rogue regime, or worse? We can only hope...              <BR>   <P>------------------<BR>I don't think so;therefore, I'm probably not.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Fformula88||12-10-2001||10:25 PM||||I do agree with the need for missle defense but I think its being sold wrong.<P>Rogue nations and terrorists could send an ICBM, but they don't have to.  Pakistan's nukes are about the size of a soccer ball.  Both the US and Russia have them in brief case size.  How do you get it here?  Ship it.  All those sealed containers that come into the country stay sealed.  Less than 1% of them are even checked before being placed on a truck and sent inland.  I do not think they will launch an ICBM.  <P>As a comprehensive national defense, its probably unrealistic.  Building a large enough system for a national defense is only likely to trigger an arms race.  I don't think a second cold war is a good idea.<P>Here is my argument for it:  Besides naming a potential terrorist or rogue state attack, how about an accidental launch?  Our Nuclear arsenal, and probably China's and Russia's are designed to launch if they loose contact with their main computers, assuming an attack happened.  If we had the technology, it would be possible to prevent an accidental nuking of a city as well as a terrorist attack.<P>I do support some kind of system, but it has to work (right now it isn't close!) and it would do no good if it caused another Nuclear arms race.||||reg||1
